  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThis strategy employs Evolutionary Strategies (ES) to optimize the overlap integral for the Erd\u0151s problem. Unlike gradient-based methods, ES directly searches the solution space using principles inspired by natural selection: mutation, selection, and adaptation. The algorithm maintains a population of candidate solutions and evolves them over generations, favoring those with better (lower) overlap integrals. \n\nThe approach is particularly suited for this non-convex problem because:\n- Mutation allows exploration of the solution space beyond local minima\n- Selection focuses computational effort on promising candidates\n- Adaptation of mutation step size helps balance exploration/exploitation\n- The repair mechanism maintains feasibility throughout the search\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Decision variable: h \u2208 [0,1]^n, where \u2211h[i] = n/2\n- Objective: min h max_k [h \u22c6 (1 - h)](k)\n- Mutation: h_new = h_parent + \u03c3 * Z, where Z \u223c N(0,I)\n- Repair: h_new[i] = clip(h_new[i], 0, 1), then scale to meet \u2211h_new = n/2\n- Selection: retain candidate with lowest overlap integral\n- Mutation step size \u03c3 adapts based on progress\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization:\n   1. If initial_h_values provided:\n      - Use as initial parent\n      - Verify feasibility (clip and scale)\n   2. Else:\n      - Create default with central pulse\n      - Normalize and clip\n\nb. ES setup:\n   1. Population size = 1 (\u03bc = 1)\n   2. Mutation step size \u03c3\u2080 = 0.1\n   3. Number of offspring \u03bb = 10\n   4. Maximum generations = 1000\n   5. Adaptation interval = 50 generations\n\nc. Evolution loop:\n   For each generation:\n   1. Generate \u03bb offspring via mutation\n   2. Evaluate fitness (negative overlap integral) for each\n   3. Repair each candidate to satisfy constraints\n   4. Select best candidate as new parent\n   5. Track best solution\n   6. Adapt \u03c3 every adaptation interval:\n      - If no improvement: \u03c3 *= 0.7\n      - Else: \u03c3 *= 0.95\n\nd. Termination:\n   - After max generations\n   - When time budget exhausted\n   - Optionally: when \u03c3 < \u03b5 (e.g., 1e-5)\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Population size: 1 (simplex, memory-efficient)\n- Mutation distribution: Gaussian with adaptive \u03c3\n- Offspring count: 10 (balance between exploration and compute)\n- Adaptation interval: 50 generations (prevent premature convergence)\n- Mutation strength decay: 0.7 when stagnated, 0.95 otherwise\n- Initial candidate: falls back to parent if initial_h_values available\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Each fitness evaluation: ~0.1s for \u03bb=10, n=280\n- 1000 generations total: ~100s\n- Adaptation mechanism ensures numerical stability\n- Repair step maintains feasibility at all times\n- Mutation bounds prevent invalid proposals\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final h must satisfy 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] = n/2\n- Evaluate C\u2085 using exact task metric\n- Return best_c5, h, and n_points\n- Intermediate repair ensures valid candidates\n- Fallback to initial_h_values if no progress",
